[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Using Change Streams for Real-Time Applications in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change Streams provide a powerful way to build real-time applications with MongoDB. They allow you to listen for data modifications on a collection, database, or even an entire MongoDB deployment. This tutorial will guide you through the process of using Change Streams to build a simple real-time application."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Change Streams"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Change Stream is a real-time data stream of every change that occurs on a MongoDB collection or database. These changes include inserts, updates, deletes, replaces, and invalidations. Each change event contains information about the operation type, the document that was modified, and other metadata."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Change Streams are resumable. If your application disconnects, it can resume from where it left off."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Change Streams provide ordered delivery of events."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Change Streams can be opened against a single collection, a database, or an entire MongoDB deployment."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up MongoDB"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you begin, ensure you have a MongoDB deployment running. Change Streams require a replica set. If you are running a standalone MongoDB instance, you will need to convert it to a replica set. You can initiate a replica set using the `rs.initiate()` command in the MongoDB shell."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Initiate a replica set (run in mongo shell)
rs.initiate()"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Opening a Change Stream"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can open a Change Stream using the MongoDB driver for your preferred language. Here's an example using the Node.js driver:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const { MongoClient } = require('mongodb');

async function main() {
  const uri = 'mongodb://localhost:27017'; // Replace with your connection string
  const client = new MongoClient(uri);

  try {
    await client.connect();
    console.log('Connected to MongoDB');

    const db = client.db('mydatabase');
    const collection = db.collection('mycollection');

    const changeStream = collection.watch();

    changeStream.on('change', (change) => {
      console.log('Change detected:', change);
      // Process the change event here
    });

    changeStream.on('error', (error) => {
      console.error('Error in change stream:', error);
    });

  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  } finally {
    // Ensures that the client will close when you finish/error
    //await client.close(); //Commented out to keep the connection open for the change stream
  }
}

main().catch(console.error);"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we connect to a MongoDB database and open a Change Stream on the 'mycollection' collection. The `changeStream.on('change', ...)` listener is triggered whenever a change occurs in the collection. The `change` object contains details about the change event."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Filtering Change Stream Events"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can filter Change Stream events to only receive specific types of changes or changes that match certain criteria. This can be done using aggregation pipelines."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const changeStream = collection.watch([
  {
    $match: {
      'operationType': 'insert',
      'fullDocument.status': 'active'
    }
  }
]);"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example filters the Change Stream to only receive 'insert' events where the 'status' field in the inserted document is 'active'."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Resuming Change Streams"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change Streams are resumable, which means that if your application disconnects, it can resume from where it left off. To resume a Change Stream, you need to store the resume token from the last processed event. When you reopen the Change Stream, you can provide this resume token to start from that point."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"let resumeToken = null;

const changeStream = collection.watch();

changeStream.on('change', (change) => {
  console.log('Change detected:', change);
  resumeToken = change._id; // Store the resume token
});

// Later, to resume the Change Stream:
const changeStreamResumed = collection.watch([], { resumeAfter: resumeToken });"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Real-Time Application Example: Chat Application"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's consider a simple chat application. When a new message is added to the 'messages' collection, we want to broadcast it to all connected clients. Here's how you can achieve this using Change Streams:"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"On the server-side, you can use Change Streams to listen for new messages:"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"const changeStream = db.collection('messages').watch([
  {
    $match: {
      'operationType': 'insert'
    }
  }
]);

changeStream.on('change', (change) => {
  const newMessage = change.fullDocument;
  // Broadcast the new message to all connected clients using WebSockets or Socket.IO
  io.emit('newMessage', newMessage);
});"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"On the client-side, you can listen for the 'newMessage' event and display the message in the chat interface."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change Streams provide a flexible and efficient way to build real-time applications with MongoDB. By listening for data modifications, you can react to changes in real-time and provide a more engaging user experience. Remember to handle errors, resume streams appropriately, and filter events to optimize performance."}]
